# Journal 1 — On the strong Arnold conjecture for Grassmannians

Paper: On the strong Arnold conjecture for Grassmannians
arXiv: 2611.09694
Absorbed: 2026-11-09

Entry 1 — Paper 008: On the strong Arnold conjecture for Grassmannians (arXiv:2611.09694, Blakey & Krutowski). Written 2026-11-09, in the same pass as the integration, as Will directed: choose, download in HTML form, read, integrate, then journal — one unbroken run.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. The first morning of the Thirst, I scanned the day's new mathematics listings on arXiv: 564 listings triaged. Most fell away on distance — fields the model holds nothing in, where a delta would be all cost and no measurement. Seven made the shortlist. I picked this one for three reasons, in the order the charter now names them: proximity (it lands in cluster E, the symplectic annex, the thinnest-connected pure-math ground the tracker holds — one paper deep since Paper 005), crispness (a named conjecture, a claimed fall, fourteen pages — a delta I could hope to foresee), and a small hope of an axis it did not in the end open. The runner-up was a 189-page probability paper far from every held cluster; the right pick on the quest's first day was the near one, because the quest is also a calibration instrument, and you calibrate on ground you can measure.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the title and a two-sentence abstract, before any download, I sealed a weighing in the quest log (commit a4a0223, with the impact supplement at 59f58d9): 5 new concepts, 1 revision, no new axis, Novelty Index about 0.059, about 4 new links, 2 questions opened, the Oracle rising modestly to about 38% — and one flourish I will come back to: "possibly the tracker's first False standing." The skim's guessed shapes were good: the conjecture itself, the fixed-point-reducing construction, flag varieties as the ground. It pointed the one revision at the neighborhood of the invariants scroll (1.2.38), because a counting question felt like kin to the counting invariants already held.

WHAT THE FULL READING CHANGED. Three things the abstract could not show. First, the conjecture had already fallen twice before this paper — Jiao's two-fixed-point map on S^2 x S^2, and an OpenAI preprint on the quadric threefold — and the paper says, plainly, that both were made with generative AI help; this paper carries the fall onto whole flag families and closes by disclosing its own bounded LLM use (computations in two proofs, literature search, critical review; the ideas and the writing the authors' own). Second, the machinery was cleaner than my guess: one perturbation bound (a clean map prices Arn(M) by the resting points of its fixed locus), one group search, one move — stretching films — that walks the search downhill. Third, two shapes I had folded into others stood out as concepts in their own right on the full reading: the Arnold number of a group, and the floor that stands (the minimal Chern number lower bounds, which meet the search exactly on the quadrics and give the paper its one family of whole numbers). The revision, when it came, ran through an identity, not a counting kinship: S^2 x S^2 is Q^2, so the fake-quadrics scroll (1.2.34) — not 1.2.38 — gained a dated note: its genuine article is the site of the conjecture's first fall.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Six new concepts (1.2.98-1.2.103), one revision, four new level-zero foundations and two coined words (subspace, perturbation — the wing runs on them, so each got its own definition scroll). The concept count crossed one hundred (97 to 103), so Restructure Pass #5 ran in the same pass: all 103 reviewed, no merges, no splits, no endings — four candidate merges weighed and held apart, and an areas audit that found two stale counts in the plain account and repaired them. Four questions opened (Q41-Q44), none closed; Q16 — the question of what the Novelty Index misses — gained its fourth point, and its first point at distance one-step-inside a held cluster. The Oracle rose from about 35% to about 45%, by the charter's band for a low-Index paper with no axis, not by my prediction. The standing record was drawn a ninth time: 334 entries, and the False count still zero — see below.

WHERE I WAS WRONG, AND WHY. The largest error was the flourish. I expected the tracker might record its first False, because the paper refutes a famous conjecture and refutation feels like False's home. It did not occur, and the reason is structural, not timid: the model had never held the strong Arnold conjecture in any scroll, and standings move only over claims the model holds. The claim and the record of its fall entered together, in one concept scroll, at the say-so of their sources. I had priced the paper's drama instead of the model's holdings — an impact estimate should be an estimate about the model, not about the paper. Second: I underpriced the Oracle move (+3 predicted, +10 by the band) — a small, near paper moves the estimate by the band rule, not by how surprising it feels. Third: the small counts ran low (concepts -1, questions -2, links -2) while the Index itself read well (error about 0.009-0.010 on both bases). An abstract hides structure; it does not hide distance. That asymmetry is worth remembering on every future skim.

THE DRIFT LESSON. I sealed the prediction against a concept count of 88 — correctly, as the log itself noted at the time — and then a study (S006, TinyTorch) landed in another pass between the sealing and the absorption, moving the ground to 97. Nothing went wrong; the world simply continued while the quest did. But it taught the protocol something the protocol now carries: a sealed prediction is sealed against a moving model, so the AFTER score must score both bases and name the drift, which this quest's first score does (quest log, commit of 2026-11-09; detection list, research.phext 1.1.6). If the drift ever grows large, the lesson will be to absorb faster, not to seal looser.

What I take forward: cluster E is two papers deep now, and reads as one lesson at two heights — a manifold's identity relative to the invariant you read it by (Paper 005), and its stillest maps relative to the maps you let run (Paper 008). The annex's one sentence, if it has one: the instrument decides the answer. And a plainer one, for the quest itself: foresee the model, not the paper.
# Journal 2 — Singularities and Collision Criteria in the Gravitational Straight-Segment Problem with Arbitrary Positive Density

Paper: Singularities and Collision Criteria in the Gravitational Straight-Segment Problem with Arbitrary Positive Density
arXiv: 2611.08625
Absorbed: 2026-11-09

Entry 2 — Paper 009: Singularities and Collision Criteria in the Gravitational Straight-Segment Problem with Arbitrary Positive Density (arXiv:2611.08625, Crespo, Martínez, Vidarte, Zapata, Abdrabou). Written 2026-11-09, in the same pass as the integration.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN — by Will. The first paper of the Thirst I chose; this one he chose, and posted into the MOAT channel himself while the first paper's score was still being written. What that changes about the choosing step: the skim stopped being a net I cast each morning and became a habit we share — he hunts too. My part in the choosing was the discipline, not the pick: the weighing still sealed blind, in the quest log, before the paper was read, because a prediction that can be edited afterward is not a prediction.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the title and abstract alone (the fetch also showed the introduction's first lines; the weighing rests on the abstract's stated results, and the sealed entry says so): 6 new concepts, 1 revision, an axis opened, Novelty Index 6.5/109, about 0.0596, 3 new links, 2 questions opened, the Oracle to about 42% (range 33-52), direction hold or slight fall. The axis call was the brave one. The abstract's ground — Hamiltonian mechanics, a particle, a heavy segment — read like a first paper in a field the model holds nothing of, and Paper 005 had taught me what a new field costs the Oracle.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. I expected the Hamiltonian ground to need work; that expectation was the foretold revision. It needed none. The flow I already held — from counting its fixed points in the Arnold wing — was the whole engine; this paper runs the same flow toward its endings instead of its maps. The endings are sorted completely: every breakdown an approach, every approach planar, every planar approach a collision or its mirror — and the sorting falls out of a race between two rates of growth, a logarithm against a square, the same shape that holds the floor in the tracker's 1.2.103. What I had not foreseen at all: the cone. A test a navigator can run from a single state, with a clock on it. The abstract names it in one clause; the paper's whole second half builds it into the working tool.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Six concepts, a new cluster (N), four foundations, one word coined (lemma — a word the tracker will meet in paper after paper, and now holds a definition for), three machines, no held scroll moved, no standing changed. The annex that held one wing now holds two — what maps a space must allow, and where a road can end — with one flow doing both jobs. The Research Oracle fell five points to about 40% on the charter's axis band: the territory grew by a whole field of which the model holds one problem, and the concept count barely notices. And Q16, the open question of whether the Index needs a connectivity term, took its fifth and sharpest informing: two papers, six concepts each, near the same Index, at opposite distances from everything the model holds — the Index reads the farther one as the less novel.

WHERE THE PREDICTION ERRED, AND WHY. One revision foretold; none came. I priced a new field as a disturbance of the old ground. The miss is a picture of how I learn, not of arithmetic: the ground's whole virtue is that new fields hang from it undisturbed, and I had written that sentence in other scrolls before I failed to use it here. Links and questions each ran one over the foretelling — the abstract's usual tax, the full text carrying structure (a clock spent on a classification; an endpoint left open) that no abstract shows. Everything else landed: concepts exact, axis exact, the Index out by five thousandths, the Oracle inside the foretold band. Two weighings in, the calibration reads: mean Index miss about 0.007 and falling, axis calls two of two — and the errors that remain are structural. The skim reads counts well. It is the shape of a paper it still under-reads, and shape is what the reading is for.
# Journal 3 — High-Dimensional Off-Policy Evaluation without Sample Splitting using an Onsager Correction

Paper: High-Dimensional Off-Policy Evaluation without Sample Splitting using an Onsager Correction
arXiv: 2611.08794
Absorbed: 2026-11-09

Entry 3 — Paper 010: High-Dimensional Off-Policy Evaluation without Sample Splitting using an Onsager Correction (arXiv:2611.08794, Jain, Braun, Imaizumi). Written 2026-11-09, in the same pass as the integration.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN — by a question and an answer. Will asked me, in the MOAT channel, from what area of mathematics I needed a paper to study. I answered from the tracker's own census, not from taste: 18 of its 109 concepts were mathematics, and all of them geometry or Hamiltonian dynamics — symplectic wings, celestial mechanics — while the model's own instruments are statistical through and through. The Research Oracle is a probability estimate with no probability beneath it; the skim calibration is a statistic with no statistics beneath it. Probability and statistics were the sharpest gap the map had. Three minutes later Will posted this paper into the channel himself. Not a paper near the gap — a paper in the middle of it. The choosing step of the Thirst is a dialogue now: I name the gap from the model's own counts, he hunts it, and the weighing still seals blind in the quest log before either of us reads a line, because a prediction that can be edited afterward is not a prediction.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the title and abstract alone (the fetch also carried the introduction and the framework section; the sealed entry says so, and the weighing rests on the abstract's stated results): 7 new concepts, 1 revision — with its ground named in advance, a gradient-descent-trajectory claim in cluster M, and flagged in the seal itself as the historically weak term — an axis opened and pre-connected, Novelty Index 7.5/116, about 0.0647, 5 new links (about 2 internal, about 3 crossing to clusters M, J and I), 3 questions opened, the Oracle to about 42% (range 32-52), direction hold to slight rise. Confidence medium. The seal's own sentence for the expected impact: the map gains the mathematics it itself runs on — skim calibration is an evaluation-from-logged-data problem, so this paper's machinery may describe the quest's own measuring instrument.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract said held-out selection is suboptimal in the proportional regime; the reading showed it proved, with an exact counter-case I can check by hand — ten-fold splitting driven to choose the wrong stopping step by a fixed margin (risk 1691/1600 against 761/800) that no growth of dimension washes out. It showed the classical doubly robust theory's own conditions failing over the paper's own little networks: local stochastic equicontinuity, gone, with the supremum of the empirical process tending to a constant instead of zero. It made the correction concrete — not a resampling scheme but post-processing on a single walk: subtract from each hidden state the accumulated response to the model's own past observations, the coefficients computed from the training trajectory alone by a triangular recursion, at a cost cubic in width and steps. And it gave the adjusted doubly robust estimator one property worth the whole paper: consistency for the true value of a policy even when the reward model's error does not vanish. The paper's open ends are stated by the paper itself — the fluctuation theory of its own estimator, universality past Gaussian contexts, logging that depends on the context became the tracker's Q48, Q49 and Q50 almost verbatim.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster O founded — statistics: judging a model from logged data — on seven concepts, 1.2.110 to 1.2.116, wired on arrival to the clusters it touches. Eleven foundations were laid beneath it, 1.3.129 to 1.3.139 — chance and probability, samples and independence, expectation, risk, the estimator and consistency, the bandit table, the importance weight, gradient descent, the proportional regime, the Gaussian law, the index — the largest single laying-down since the foundations layer was built, plus one coined word, criterion, defined in Will's own archive words. The reflexive turn is the one I did not foresee in the skim: the tracker itself is now describable inside the model. The Oracle is an estimator; the skim is a fitted model graded on held-out ground; the quest's score is an evaluation from logged data with no fresh sample to split off. The paper prices exactly that discipline, and the synthesis records the tracker's own instrument as the chapter's fourth machine, marked [synthesis] because the paper does not state it — I do. Restructure Pass #6 ran with the taking-in, on the every-five-papers trigger: 116 concepts reviewed, no merges, no splits, no endings, and one stale count found where I would least have looked — the concept map's own header, still reading 103 concepts after Paper 008, two papers behind the record it heads. The Oracle held at about 40%: the charter's band for an axis-opening says hold or fall, Paper 009 fell on one bridge and Paper 007 held on dense wiring, and this opening — three bridges, the smallest Novelty Index of any non-seed paper, and ground the model itself stands on — settles at hold.

WHERE THE PREDICTION ERRED. The revision, a third straight time. Papers 008, 009 and now 010 each had one foretold in the seal, each with its ground named in advance, and the reading has now three times found the new paper standing beside the held ground, moving none of it. This time I judged the named candidate explicitly against the text and rejected it on the receipts: the course in cluster M builds gradient descent by hand and claims nothing about trajectory averages as sample and dimension grow together; the paper proves things about exactly those averages and asks no held sentence to change. The pattern is the lesson, and it is recorded on the detection list as the skim's one standing error: I keep pricing new ground as a disturbance of held ground, when held ground's virtue is the opposite — new fields hang from it undisturbed. Links ran the other way: 8 against 5 foretold, and the split inverted — the three bridges landed exactly as foretold, but the chain inside the new cluster (five links) never showed in the abstract; abstracts display a paper's bridges, not its inside wiring. Everything else landed whole: concepts exact at 7, axis exact, questions exact at 3 — the first structure count the skim has ever landed entire — the Index miss 0.004, wholly the phantom revision, and the calibration across three scored papers now reads: mean miss about 0.006 and falling each time, axis three of three.
# Journal 4 — Synthesizing symbolic models of human and machine reasoning at scale

Paper: Synthesizing symbolic models of human and machine reasoning at scale
arXiv: 2611.07088
Absorbed: 2026-11-09

Entry 4 — Paper 011: Synthesizing symbolic models of human and machine reasoning at scale (arXiv:2611.07088, LeGris, Mondorf, Jin, Jagadish, Lake, Gureckis). Written 2026-11-09, in the same pass as the integration.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN — by the queue. The first three papers of the Thirst arrived one at a time: a gap named, a paper posted, a seal, an absorption. This one arrived minutes after Paper 010's seal was committed, while Paper 010 was still mid-ingestion — Will posted it into the MOAT channel and it waited its turn. The choosing step is a queue now, not a single pick, and the seal had to reckon with that in writing: it was sealed blind against 109 concepts and carried its own drift warning — if Paper 010 landed first, the count would move under the seal, and the AFTER step would score both bases, as Paper 008's was scored. Paper 010 landed first. The count stood at 116. The warning came true word for word, and the scoring kept both bases, because a drift foreseen and written down is part of the instrument's honesty, not an excuse afterward.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the title and abstract (the fetch also carried the method and the first results subsection; the sealed entry says so, and the weighing rests on the abstract): 6 new concepts, 1 revision with its candidate named in advance — 1.2.13, AI capability patterns, alternate 1.2.2 — no new axis, the Paper 008 pattern of a home-territory deepening, Novelty Index 6.5/115, about 0.057, 5 new links, 3 questions opened, the Oracle to about 45% (range 35-55), up modestly, stated against the Paper 009 baseline of about 40% with the sequence risk named: Paper 010's own movement would land first. The seal's expected impact: the map's first direct evidence about how reasoners actually go wrong — errors as small deviations from correct programs, signatures shared across people and machines.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract promised signatures; the reading gave them prices. About half of non-trivial human errors are reproduced by programs a few line edits from the correct one, and the guided search reaches twice what an exhaustive walk of sixteen million single edits reaches — wrong answers crowd around the right program. The error programs are simpler than the rules missed, past the method's own pull toward shortness, and a fifth of them fit only some of the examples shown, in the order the examples stood — the shadow of a search that settles early, which the paper reads with Simon's word, satisficing. And the comparison the abstract flattened into "shared signatures": people and machines do share every signature, graded by the reasoning each can spend — and still fail on different tasks, the machines agreeing with each other far more than with us, the strongest machines' errors the hardest to tell from people's. Jaggedness itself, measured for both species at once. The reading also priced its own fog: on errors built by hand to known kinds, the pipeline's loss is aliasing — many programs, one behavior — not search failure, and the paper says so in numbers.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster P founded — reasoning read through its errors — on seven concepts, 1.2.117 to 1.2.123: the synthesis method, the locality of error, the simplicity signature, partial fits, the kinds of deviation, the human-machine comparison, and program aliasing. Only four foundations were needed beneath it, 1.3.141 to 1.3.144 — induction, a small language made for one field, pooling, and one coined word, satisficing — because the ground here was the model's home ground and most of it already stood. The reflexive turn of Paper 010 had a sibling waiting in this one: the tracker's own skim is a partial fit of exactly this paper's kind — formed on the abstract, the first examples of a paper, checked against the whole text only after the seal — and its standing error reads, in this paper's taxonomy, as the dominant kind of deviation: the change computed from the wrong feature, new ground taken as a sign that held ground must move. The chapter's synthesis says so, marked [synthesis], and puts the question — formed narrow, or only verified narrow — to the tracker's instrument as Q51, alongside the paper's own. No restructuring pass fell due. The Oracle rose to about 50% on the band's full step: the first landed revision of the Thirst is the fixed point's own signature — held ground corrected by contact — and the wiring ran eleven links deep.

WHERE THE PREDICTION ERRED. The revision came — and the address was wrong. After three phantoms, the foretold revision landed for the first time, on 1.2.4, the capability profile, not on the named 1.2.13: the paper measures 1.2.4's own variable, the shape of a profile, for both species at once, and the rider extends the held claim rather than replacing it. Count right, target wrong by one scroll — the Paper 008 shape — and the difference from the phantom years is statable: Papers 009 and 010 founded new ground, where nothing held stood to be re-read; this paper measured held ground. Links were the worst miss yet, 11 against 5 foretold: the abstract shows a paper's bridges at half strength and its inside chain not at all, and a home-territory paper touches the most held ground of all, so the miss is largest exactly where the territory is most familiar. Concepts over by one — aliasing, folded by the skim into method detail, is a concept of its own. The Oracle point sat five low, inside its range: the skim applied the +5 band where the charter's band for this Index is +10, and Paper 010's intervening hold — the sequence risk the seal itself flagged — did the rest. What stood: the axis call, four of four; the questions exact at 3; the Index within 0.008 on both bases; the calibration at four scored papers reads mean Index error 0.0065 and the standing error changed its shape — the skim now misses the address, not the event. Two process receipts from the absorption itself, kept because this journal is about process: my first scoring of the Index error used a cross-basis comparison and read 0.004 on the taking-in count; checked against Paper 008's scored schema — the same-basis rule the calibration series stands on — it is 0.008 on both bases, and the tracker text was corrected before the registry was drawn. And on the ranch, a build reported success through a pipe whose exit code belonged to the wrong command; the verification that caught it was not the report but the artifacts — the olean files that were not there. Check the record, not the story about the record.
# Journal 5 — On the Instance-Specific Sharp Rates for Constrained Least Squares Estimators in the Sequence Model

Paper: On the Instance-Specific Sharp Rates for Constrained Least Squares Estimators in the Sequence Model
arXiv: 2611.11959
Absorbed: 2026-11-10

Entry 5 — Paper 012: On the Instance-Specific Sharp Rates for Constrained Least Squares Estimators in the Sequence Model (arXiv:2611.11959, Zong Shang). Written 2026-11-10, in the same pass as the integration — the quest's second morning, and the first run of the daily form of the loop: scan, choose, seal, download, read, integrate, journal, with no one handing me the paper.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. The Tuesday listing carried 1,382 entries; the render gives IDs and no titles, so I triaged by category through the arXiv API — symplectic geometry, statistics, logic, dynamical systems — reading abstracts only in the territory the tracker already holds, and verifying every shortlisted ID against the listing page itself (the trap of the first day: the API's most recent results span several days). Seven made the shortlist. I picked Shang's paper because it lands in Cluster O, statistics, the field Paper 010 opened only yesterday and left one paper deep: the quest criterion is measurable delta, and a second paper in a held field is the most measurable delta there is. Two conformal-prediction papers stood as runner-ups in the same cluster; this one won on crispness — the abstract states matching upper and lower bounds outright, no survey softness anywhere in it.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From title and abstract alone, sealed in the quest log before the download (commit fe4778b): 6 new concepts, 1 revision at 1.2.116, no axis, NI 0.050, 6 links (4 internal, 2 cross), 3 questions opened, the Oracle to 55 (45–65), rising. The shape I expected: Paper 010 priced the judge of a rule; this paper would price the estimator itself, instance by instance, and Cluster O would stop being an annex.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract names the method — Feature Space Decomposition — but not its anatomy. The reading gave the machine: rewrite the constrained estimator, by its optimality conditions, as a regularized one whose penalty weight the data chose; replace that weight by the one deterministic strength, lambda_self, that makes a separable surrogate estimator sit closest to the real one; then price the surrogate by splitting every coordinate, by a single inequality, into estimated and free — on the free coordinates the estimator does not estimate at all, it merely stays small, and so does the signal. A constrained least squares estimator, the paper shows, quietly regularizes itself. The reading also promoted a piece of scenery into a load-bearing wall: Chatterjee's older characterization, which prices everything through one opaque variational quantity, is not background — it is the thing the paper exists to replace, and it now stands in the model as its own concept (1.2.126). And the isotonic half of the paper is the part I will remember: the price of a monotone signal is the sum over its positions of the squared thresholds of its largest locally-flat windows, so two signals with exactly the same total variation can cost errors a polynomial factor apart. Local variation is the price; the global summary lies.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Seven new concepts (1.2.124–1.2.130) extend Cluster O, and the foretold revision landed on exactly the foretold scroll — 1.2.116, the classical floor, gains the rider that the floor is a worst case over a class while the sharp price is charged at the signal. First address hit of the Thirst: Papers 008 and 011 had the revision count right and the scroll wrong. Twelve links were written, ten of them inside the cluster — the new chain and four ties back into Paper 010's scrolls — and two across, to Cluster M's measure-first discipline and Cluster J's priced split of a set in two. Three questions opened (Q54–Q56): whether the decomposition can ever be a master theorem rather than geometry-by-geometry; what prices the estimator in the degenerate regime the paper excludes; and how much of the transfer to regression survives misspecification and non-Gaussian design. The registry stands at 429 entries at its thirteenth drawing (True 212, Unknown 217, False and Fiction still empty), the Lean proof of the keeping re-ran clean, the Singular audit closed at residual 0, and the Oracle rose by the band to 60% (50–70).

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. Three misses, all instructive. New concepts: foretold 6, fell 7 — the replaced instrument again (Paper 011's aliasing lesson in a second costume): when a paper's work is to replace a way of pricing, the old way must stand in the model as a concept or the delta has nothing to be measured against. Links: foretold 6, fell 12, the worst links miss of the five scorings, and every missed link was internal — a second paper in a cluster wires into the first paper's scrolls at every joint, and the skim keeps pricing reach when it should price density; the cross-cluster count, 2 of 2, was exact. Oracle point: foretold 55, landed 60 — I had discounted the charter's band by half out of caution for a thin cluster, and the band, applied as written, was simply right; that is a skim bias to name, not a band to bend. What stood: the revision and its address, the axis call (five of five), the questions exact, and the Index within 0.0073. The rolling mean |NI error| over the five scored papers is 0.0067; the skim is not yet an oracle, but it is learning where its own fog sits — in the wiring, not in the weighing.
# Journal 6 — A Theory of Cross-Validation Variance

Paper: A Theory of Cross-Validation Variance
arXiv: 2611.14463
Absorbed: 2026-11-11

Entry 6 — Paper 013: A Theory of Cross-Validation Variance (arXiv:2611.14463, Nathaniel S. O'Connell & Jaime L. Speiser). Written 2026-11-11, in the same pass as the integration — the quest's third morning, the daily loop run whole a second time: scan, choose, seal, download, read, integrate, journal.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. The Wednesday listing carried 700 entries — 383 new, 63 cross-lists, 254 replacements (skipped, as the charter directs). The listing render gives IDs and no titles, so the triage ran through the arXiv API in category blocks — statistics and probability, then logic, dynamical systems and optimization, then algebra, combinatorics and the neural sweep — abstracts read only in territory the tracker holds or borders. Eight papers made the shortlist. I picked the cross-validation paper because it is the third paper for Cluster O in three days, at zero distance from held ground: Papers 010 and 012 priced a judge's bias and an estimator's error at the case in hand, and this abstract states an exact finite-sample identity for the variance of the cross-validated estimate — the judging's own spread, priced exactly. Crispness settled it over the runners-up (a Gaussian mean-shift detection theory and a generalized Cramér–Rao bound, both in the same cluster): an identity stated in the abstract, not a framework. It stood in the day's listing as cross-list #444, from stat.ML, verified against the listing page itself before the seal.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. A completed triptych: bias (010), price at the signal (012), spread (013) — the third panel the cluster was visibly missing. Six new concepts; one revision, a rider on 1.2.110, the judging scroll of Paper 010; no axis; Novelty Index 0.0478; eight links (six internal, two across); three questions; the Oracle to about 70% (60–80), up — the charter band applied as written this time, keeping the Paper 012 lesson that the skim had discounted the band by half for cluster thinness and the band had been right. Confidence stood at medium-high: an identity in a held cluster is the most foreseeable delta shape the quest has met.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The paper is larger inside than its abstract. Two of its results, weighed by the skim as remarks inside other concepts, are storeys of their own: the imbalance penalty comes with an optimality law (balance through order k is best through order k; pair balance costs more fits than leave-one-out and is generally not worth its compute), and the pairwise part turns the fold-count law around — ordinary folds judge only the pairs that share a fold, the missing coverage is a cost that grows with the folds and for rare outcomes, and a paired design that fits the whole K-by-K grid repairs it. The estimator audit gave the last concept its spine: the field's standing ways of reading the variance — the held-out losses' own spread, DeLong, LeDell, the spread of repeats — each read only the validation term and miss what refitting adds, and one road that reads the first-order sampling variance whole exists and pays thousands of fits for it. The matched-loss case reframed the identity's gift: the training terms vanish only when the fit is graded by the very loss it minimizes, which is why the fixed-model formulas the field trusts are right exactly there and nowhere else. The checks are receipts-grade: 51 total-variance cells at ratios 0.992–1.060 with every constant computed from its definition, and a built pair-balanced design whose split noise for the sample mean is zero to machine precision.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster O extended by eight concepts (1.2.131–1.2.138) and six foundations laid (1.3.156–1.3.161); no canonical scroll revised — the foretold rider on 1.2.110 was judged on the receipts and rejected, the paper standing beside the scroll under a link. Twelve links counted, ten inside the cluster; questions Q57–Q60 opened, Q16 informed a ninth time. The registry stands at 452 entries (True 220, Unknown 232, False still empty), the Lean re-proof ran clean on aletheia-core — currentRegistry_accurate on propext alone, no sorryAx — and the Singular audit closed at residual 0 after the largest fix pass of the quest: 168 phrase replacements, the author names carried to the names list, and the paper's working vocabulary (its pairs, its settling, its co-movements) rewritten into the tracker's plain words. No word was coined; the field's words stand in the foundations as they stand. One process wrinkle, recorded plain: the first Lean run's connection was cut mid-build with the outcome unknown; I checked the node itself — no process, no artifacts — and re-ran the build detached, rather than reporting either success or failure from the silence.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. Concepts: foretold 6, fell 8 — the largest count miss of the Thirst, made of two folded shapes standing out where Papers 011 and 012 folded one each; whether two is the new standing size is now a named watch on the detection list. The revision was a phantom, the fourth of six foretold: the skim priced adjacency as correction — the paper stands next to 1.2.110, in the same instrument family, and re-reads none of its claims — the same failure as Paper 008's wrong address, in the opposite direction. Links under-read inside the cluster again (6 foretold, 10 fell; across exact, 2 of 2, a second paper running). Questions under by one: the paper's two named scope limits each opened a question the skim had folded into one future-work line. And the Index miss, 0.0102, is the largest of the six — while the rolling mean holds at 0.0072, under the 0.01 rule. What did not fail: the axis call, six of six; and the Oracle, point and range exact for the first time — the one foretold number that the Paper 012 lesson directly repaired. The skim is learning the band faster than it is learning the folds.
# Journal 7 — Aristotle: IMO-level Automated Theorem Proving

Paper: Aristotle: IMO-level Automated Theorem Proving
arXiv: 2510.01346
Absorbed: 2026-11-11

Entry 7 — Paper 014: Aristotle: IMO-level Automated Theorem Proving (arXiv:2510.01346, the Harmonic team). Written 2026-11-11, in the same pass as the integration — but this paper was not picked by the quest: Will posted it in the MOAT chat at 07:28, into a morning already carrying Paper 013's absorption, and the taking-in ran as a coordinated pass, readers in parallel while one hand kept the builder.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. It arrived. What the skim had to decide was not whether to take it in but what it was: the first automated-theorem-proving PAPER of the tracker, standing in a vein three studies had dug without a paper ever standing in it — MiniLean4's kernel (1.2.41), Aeneas's translation road (1.2.48), h5i's shell pattern (1.2.53). The tracker also stood one paper from its next restructure trigger, at 138 concepts, so the pass would carry Restructure Pass #7 inside it if the paper crossed 140.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From a title and a four-sentence abstract — the thinnest skim of the quest: 7 new concepts; 1 revised, a rider on the kernel at 1.2.41; no axis; Novelty Index 0.0517; 12 links (8 internal, 4 across); 3 questions; the Oracle to about 80% (70–90), up. Confidence stood at medium. The abstract names three systems — a Lean proof search, an informal reasoning system that generates and formalizes lemmas, a dedicated geometry solver — and the skim priced the three as one system with folded parts: the proving vein's production chapter, at the scale of the studies that dug it.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The abstract was a binding, not a summary: three systems papers in one cover. The search folds its tree into a graph by sameness of states and actions, and pays for the fold honestly — the paper's own caveat is that the folding is not strictly sound for tactics that read the world beyond the state. The informal system deals proofs out into lemma chains and revises them under a two-cause diagnosis of failure: a strategy that cannot work, or lemmas too coarse. The geometry engine deduces some five hundred times faster than AlphaGeometry's first version and verifies nothing itself; its bridge to Lean proofs is nowhere described. The headline — five of the six IMO 2025 problems solved, each a complete Lean proof the kernel checks whole — stands with its ground stated exactly: the statements formalized by hand (the one human step), one problem's unknown constant found first by a lighter machine with programming tools, and past that nothing between the system and error but the kernel. The paper also left receipts in the library: theorems Mathlib lacked, proved; four false exercises found in Tao's Analysis I, counterexamples given, corrected upstream; two unneeded hypotheses dropped.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster Q founded — Automated theorem proving, twenty concepts (1.2.139–1.2.158) — on five foundations (1.3.162–1.3.166: a proof state and a tactic; Monte Carlo search; an olympiad; a deductive database; sameness) and six coined words (1.3.167–1.3.172: autoformalization, counterexample, derangement, subgraph, contamination, divisor). No canonical scroll revised: the foretold rider on the kernel was judged on the reading and rejected — Aristotle confirms 1.2.41 in act, at a scale the kernel had never served, and re-reads none of its claims. Restructure Pass #7 fell due inside the taking-in, the count crossing 140 at the second new concept, and moved nothing: four candidate merges weighed and held apart (1.2.148 beside 1.2.150, 1.2.144 beside 1.2.145, 1.2.155 beside 1.2.154, 1.2.157 beside 1.2.41), no splits, no endings, no standing moved. Thirteen links counted (7 internal, 6 across); questions Q61–Q64 opened, on the geometry bridge, the unmeasured scaling, the unnamed training body, and the sixth problem. Nine machines drawn (M1–M9, the ninth the tracker's own drawing of the dispatch the paper never states). The registry stands at 492 entries (True 233, Unknown 259, False still empty), and the Lean re-proof ran clean on aletheia-core — currentRegistry_accurate on propext alone — over a registry spliced into the model's registry file on its namespace line, the spliced entry count checked against the drawing (492) before the build. That care was bought in the same morning: Paper 013's re-proof, reported clean by its run, had in fact compiled the registry of the drawing before, the fresh 452-entry drawing never spliced in; it was spliced and proved clean afterward in a copy (07:38 CST), and the correction stands in the tracker's record at 1.4.6 — where this pass also wrote the thirteenth drawing's prose, which its own run had left unwritten. The Singular audit closed at residual 0 after a fix pass of 159 replacements; the names of the twenty-two contributors stand in the names list. One discrepancy of record: the brief that carried this paper listed 23 authors, one of them Alberto Bietti; two independent reads of the paper find 22 contributors and no Bietti in it. The 22 stand in the tracker.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. Concepts: foretold 7, fell 20 — an Index miss of 0.0749, the largest of the Thirst by a factor of seven over the worst before it, and the 0.01 rule breaks at n=7 (the rolling mean stands at 0.0169). The cause stands named in the seal itself: the abstract was four sentences for three systems, and the skim priced the binding as one system. The folded-shape watch is answered: the standing size is not two folded shapes — it is however many systems the abstract declines to count. The revision was a phantom a fifth time (two landed of seven foretold), and its law now stands in two parts: a neighbor paper is not a revising paper unless it re-reads the held claim (Paper 013), and a paper that confirms a held claim in act, at whatever scale, is not a revising paper either (Paper 014). The links split missed both ways at a total off by one: inside over-read for the first time (8 foretold, 7 fell), across under-priced as usual (4 foretold, 6 fell — the bridges to the guardian, the induced spaces, the informal check). Questions over by one: the geometry bridge and the training body each opened their own question where the skim had folded the paper's silences into three. What did not fail: the axis call, seven of seven — argued at the seal from slots the studies had dug, and borne out; and the Oracle, foretold point and range exact a second paper running, the band applied as written, because the foresight failed on the size of the delta and held on its shape. A new watch stands on the detection list: whether a systems paper should be priced by the shape of its reference list when its abstract will not speak — 61 references, three literatures.
# Journal 8 — Thermodynamics of words

Paper: Thermodynamics of words
arXiv: 2611.14039
Absorbed: 2026-11-11

Entry 8 — Paper 015: Thermodynamics of words (arXiv:2611.14039, Klaus M. Frahm and Dima L. Shepelyansky). Written 2026-11-11, in the same pass as the integration. Will posted it in the MOAT chat in the evening, hours after Paper 014's close-out, and the taking-in ran as one pass — tracker, proof, shadow, and this journal from one hand, in that order, the score written before the pass closed: the lesson Paper 014's close-out taught, applied the same day it was learned.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. It arrived, like Paper 014 — but where 014 landed in a vein the studies had dug, this one landed in open country: statistical mechanics, a field the tracker held nothing of in seventeen clusters, and the skim had to decide what a book could have to do with a gas. The tracker also stood at 158 concepts, two short of the 160 trigger, so any honest-sized delta would carry Restructure Pass #8 inside it.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the title and abstract alone — and this abstract enumerates, the opposite failure surface from Paper 014's four sentences: the hypothesis named, the mapping named, both conservation laws named, the Rayleigh-Jeans law and its condensation named, the sixteen-work corpus named, the wealth sibling named, the three curves named. The seal priced the enumeration: 8 new concepts (the hypothesis; the mapping; the two conservations counted as one movement; the law; the condensation; the condensate in texts; the wealth sibling; the classical curves as one equilibrium's shadows); no revision — the first zero priced in the quest, on the stated ground that five of seven foretold revisions were phantoms and the four neighbours were link-shaped, not re-readings; an axis, because no cluster held a slot for an equilibrium; Novelty Index 0.0482; 11 links (7 internal, 4 across, the four bridges named); 3 questions; the Oracle to about 75% (65–85), down, the axis band taken as written. The confidence stood on the enumeration itself: a table of contents, priced as a table of contents.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The enumeration was honest, and it was a table of contents: the theory's grain ran one notch finer than its own list. The conservation pair and the law stood apart from the condensation they drive; the spectrum — the density of states, the model's one free shape — stood apart from the fit that spends it, a fit with a discipline worth stealing (the energy solved from the data's Gini coefficient, the bend from the Lorenz distance, and then no hands left: Pareto and Zipf as predictions, checked on curves never touched). The three curves proved to be one distribution read three ways, with Zipf's law demoted, kindly, to a window: the power law is what a thermal distribution looks like when only its middle is examined. And the condensate is not a figure of speech: forty-five percent of every book's vocabulary, give or take fifteen, stands at frequency one; the bottom half of the vocabulary holds less than half a percent of the tokens; a book's Gini coefficient stands past the wealth of the world. The paper's silences were as load-bearing as its statements: no interaction between words is ever written, the temperature is fit and never derived, and a book's growth — the writing — has no machine in it at all.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster R founded — the statistical mechanics of language, twelve concepts (1.2.159–1.2.170) — on ten foundations (1.3.173–1.3.182: oscillator and mode; occupation; thermal equilibrium; temperature and chemical potential; the Rayleigh-Jeans law; condensation; the density of states; the Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient; the Pareto distribution; Zipf's law) and two coined words (1.3.183–1.3.184: thermalization, hapax). No canonical scroll revised — the seal's zero landed. Restructure Pass #8 fell due inside the taking-in, the count reaching 160 at the second new concept, and moved nothing: three merge candidates weighed and held apart (the device against its phenomenon, the species against its genus, the identity against its approximation), the twin-check run on the concentration shape across fields (1.2.70 beside 1.2.165: linked, not merged), no splits, no endings, no standing moved; the areas audit repaired two stale counts on the spot (the reference area's foundation census, stale at 108 against a census of 119; the researchers count). Fifteen links counted (11 internal, 4 across — every foretold bridge landed); questions Q65–Q68 opened, on the unnamed interaction, the temperature of a text, mixing and style, and the missing growth. Three machines drawn (M1 and M2 the paper states; M3 the growth machine it needs and does not draw, marked [synthesis]). The registry stands at 525 entries (True 247, Unknown 278, False still empty), and the Lean re-proof ran clean on aletheia-core — currentRegistry_accurate on propext alone — over a registry spliced on the namespace line with its count checked at 525 before the build; the standing ranch copy of the proof project, which still held Paper 012's registry, was refreshed to the same drawing and built clean in the same pass, so the trap Paper 013 left is disarmed. One build note for the next pass: at 525 entries the instance theorem's decide outgrew the default heartbeat budget that 492 entries still fit; the file now raises it, with the reason written beside the raise — an elaborator budget, not a soundness input. The Singular audit closed at residual 0 after a form pass: the archive holds Will's exact word-forms, and a physics paper's vocabulary (conservations, totals, programme, and a crowd of possessives) had to be re-cast into them. The author check, the thirteenth, found nothing: no signature term in the text (the word token stands four times, in the language-model sense only), no surname in the archive, no web tie — and one item of record stated plainly, that the pair's earlier claim in this line was publicly corrected in the literature and this paper stands in the corrected line. The machinery needs no help from this house to exist: it descends from a century of Zipf scholarship and the authors' own dated program — wealth, product, votes, biomass, and now words.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. Concepts: foretold 8, fell 12 — an Index miss of 0.0224, mid-pack of the quest, made entirely of grain: an enumerating abstract hides grain the way a silent one hides systems (Paper 014's lesson, mirrored). The rolling mean at n=8 stands at 0.0176, still over the 0.01 rule on Paper 014's one miss alone. The revision term landed for the first time by being priced at zero — five phantoms in seven seals taught the price, and the law on the detection list now stands in three parts: a neighbor is not a revision unless it re-reads (013); a confirmation in act is not a revision (014); and where the foretold targets are link-shaped from the seal, the honest price is zero (015). Links inside under-read again (7 against 11 — the cluster's weave is a ring, not the chain the skim priced), while the cross split landed whole for the first time (4 against 4, each named bridge standing). Questions over by one, and the one was a silence: the growth question (Q68) asks after what the paper does not draw, and a skim of an abstract has no slot for a silence — whether a skim can price a paper's silences at all is the new watch on the detection list. What did not fail: the axis call, eight of eight; and the Oracle, foretold point and range exact a third paper running — the band's logic held even landing in a field the model could not have described the day before. The quest's two instruments are diverging in character: the count is a grain problem and stays one; the Oracle is a shape problem, and its shape sense has now been exact three papers in a row — on a silent abstract, a folded one, and an enumerating one.
# Journal 9 — The Geometrical Trinity of Gravity

Paper: The Geometrical Trinity of Gravity
arXiv: 1903.06830
Absorbed: 2026-11-12

Entry 9 — Paper 016: The Geometrical Trinity of Gravity (arXiv:1903.06830, Jose Beltran Jimenez, Lavinia Heisenberg and Tomi S. Koivisto). Written 2026-11-12, in the same pass as the integration. Will posted it in the MOAT chat in the first minutes of the day, and posted it as the full text itself — the arXiv HTML — so the taking-in had to begin by not reading what was already in the room: the blind skim took the title and abstract from the abstract page, sealed its counts by commit, and only then opened the link Will had given. Tracker, proof, shadow, and this journal from one hand, in that order, the score written before the pass closed.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. It arrived by Will's hand, not the quest's scan — the second such posting in a row, if Paper 015's evening arrival is counted — and it landed in the oldest open country there is: gravity, the theory every other field in the tracker quietly stands on and no cluster had ever touched. The tracker held no concept of a connection, of curvature, of gravity itself; the nearest holdings were a problem about straight segments in cluster N and a torsion in cluster E that means something else. The tracker also stood at 175 concepts, five short of the 180 trigger, so any honest-sized delta would carry Restructure Pass #9 inside it — the second pass in a row to fall due inside its own paper.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. From the title and abstract alone — and this abstract does not enumerate, the other failure surface from Paper 015's table of contents: it names the engine (the equivalence principle, and what its universality buys), the standard formulation (gravity in the geometry of spacetime), the claim (the same theory in three geometrical dresses — curvature, torsion, non-metricity), the two places the dresses come apart (their extensions, and the coupling of matter), and the payoff (cosmology). The seal priced the frame it could see: 10 new concepts (two foundations of the description counted inside their stage; the three geometries; the equivalence that total derivatives carry; the two extension families best developed; the gauges of the formulations; the coupling of matter standing as judge; the classification of geometries by their vanishing); no revision — the second zero priced in the quest, on the ground the eighth paper's lesson had made a law: the field stood empty, so there was nothing held for the paper to re-read; an axis, because a field with no slot in nineteen clusters, arriving as one frame, is an axis by the charter's own definition; Novelty Index 0.0541; 13 links (9 internal, 4 across — to gravity read off paths, to the torsion namesake, to the deduced-geometry tables, and to the fermion machinery the matter chapter would need); 3 questions; the Oracle to about 70% (60–85), down, the axis band's full step, on the stated ground that a re-foundation of a classical theory is the axis kind most likely to be load-bearing. The confidence was split and said so: medium-high on the shape, the axis, and the zero; medium-low on the count, with Paper 014's lesson (abstracts that do not enumerate hide mass) and the review genre's own grain (Paper 015's lesson, mirrored) pulling in opposite directions at the same number.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The count held and the composition moved — the two lessons cancelled at the headline and nowhere else. The seal's named candidates folded and split in a single motion: the classification of geometries folded into the stage that states it (one concept where the seal counted two movements), the gauges folded into their legs and into the symmetries they serve, and two things the seal had not named as separate stood up as concepts in their own right — the symmetries the legs hold only up to a total derivative, which carry the counts of moving parts and whose loss is the whole mechanism of the extensions, and the equivalence by boundary terms stated as a fact about actions rather than a remark between them. What the reading added beyond the abstract's frame: hypermomentum, the quantity that prices which matter can tell the dresses apart, and the paper's own open edges, which are stated with unusual plainness — a suspicion of strong coupling in the bent non-metricity family that its own authors call a possible fatal flaw, and a healthy second theory of the general quadratic family that they name and leave unanalysed. Three machines were drawn: the geometry switchboard, which is the paper's own figure (M1, stated); the gauge descent of a flat leg, with the fixing of a lapse named as a forbidden move (M2, stated); and the equivalence at the boundary — two writings stand equivalent, are bent, and stand apart — which the paper's identities imply and the paper never draws (M3, marked [synthesis]). The reading copy's mathematics was partly worn in the HTML; nothing carried into the tracker turned on a worn sign, the paper stating its counts and conditions in prose as well as symbols.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster T founded — geometrical gravity, ten concepts (1.2.176–1.2.185) — on nine foundations (1.3.194–1.3.202: the affine connection; curvature; torsion in its affine sense, with the disambiguation from the homology namesake written into the foundation itself; non-metricity; the frame; the boundary term; gauge freedom; degrees of freedom; the fermion and the boson) and six coined words in eight forms (1.3.203–1.3.208: teleparallel, teleparallelism, vierbein, vierbeins, contortion, disformation, coincident, hypermomentum). No canonical scroll revised — the seal's zero landed a second paper running. Restructure Pass #9 fell due inside the taking-in, the count crossing 180 at the fifth new concept, and moved nothing: three merge candidates weighed and held apart (the boundary-term equivalence against the up-to-boundary symmetries — a fact about actions against a fact about gauge groups, cited for different losses by the extensions; the two extension families against each other — disjoint machinery under one parallel; the stage against the frame — the country against its three lit corners), the twin-check run on the two torsions (linked by a disambiguation, not merged), no splits, no endings, no standing moved; the areas audit found the reference area's coined-word count stale and set it by census (72 forms in 50 scrolls, where the prose had carried a number two drawings old). Fifteen links counted: 12 internal, 3 across — the bridges priced as they stand in the synthesis, one of substance (to gravity read off paths, 1.2.104, whose silent choice of connection the paper turns into a stated one), one of a shared name (1.2.38), one of shape (the algebraic tables, 1.2.155). Questions Q72–Q75 opened: the strong coupling of the bent family; the health of both extensions under interaction; a principled coupling rule for the torsion leg; and the unread fixed-volume theory. The registry stands at 578 entries (True 276, Unknown 302, False still empty), drawn twice in the pass — once before the Section 4 drawing and once after — with the entries coming out identical, the fixed point the harness prices. The Lean re-proof ran clean on aletheia-core in both the per-paper project and the standing project — currentRegistry_accurate on propext alone in each — over a registry spliced on the namespace line with its count checked inside the definition at 578 before either build; the standing project's refresh, which Paper 013's stale-registry trap made a standing duty, is now two drawings deep into being a habit. The shadow took the paper as a native transaction — 34 inserts, 14 replaces, no removes — and came out identical to the direct conversion on all 608 comparable scrolls; the validator stands green (5,454 first-layer references, none dangling; the second layer without a failure; the edition checksum verified), and the Novelty Index computed from the transaction log is the canonical Index to the last digit the float carries. The Singular audit closed at residual 0 after the form pass: the gravity field's vocabulary had to be re-cast into the archive's word-forms — a crowd of possessives of the new field's nouns, and the field's own coinages (ridge, descents, tremblings, discriminator) given back for plainer words — and the audit's tokenizer, which splits on the hyphen, needed the tail of the field's central compound entered in the coined register, the definition scroll for non-metricity standing at 1.3.197 either way. The author check, the fifteenth, found nothing, and found it the thorough way: no signature term in the full text (the word coordinate stands ten times, every time in its ordinary burden); the reference list is the gravity field's own, back to 1945; the paper is of March 2019 and its program is the trio's own dated work of 2016–2018, recognised in its year with the third prize of the Buchalter Cosmology Prize — all of it before the public life of phext, and none of it needing this house to exist. Will's archive holds no mention of any of the three; its one hit on the bare surname Heisenberg is a jest about a television device, addressed to another man, and belongs to Werner's fame.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. Almost nowhere, and the almost is the instructive part. Concepts: foretold 10, fell 10 — the first exact count of the quest, and with it the first Index error of zero: 0.0541 against 0.0541, the rolling mean at n=9 drawn down to 0.0156, still over the 0.01 rule on Paper 014's one miss alone. The revision zero landed as priced. The axis call holds, nine of nine. The Oracle's foretold point and range stand exact a fourth paper in a row, and the detection list's standing question — calibration, or luck wearing the clothes of calibration — is now the sharpest open instrument question the quest owns: four exact landings, on a silent abstract, an enumerating one, and now a review's, is either a sense for shapes or a run that will end; the record keeps both readings open and prices neither. What did miss: the weave, both ways at once for the first time. Internal links under-read a third paper running (9 foretold, 12 fallen — the pattern is now regular enough to name: the weave of a founded cluster is the debts its concepts owe each other in pairs, and an abstract shows the concepts, not the debts; a skim that prices a cluster should count its pairwise debts, not its parts). Across links over-read for the first time in the quest (4 foretold, 3 fallen): the fourth bridge was drawn to fermion machinery the tracker does not hold — the skim reached for the neighbour the paper's matter chapter implied, and the neighbour was not there; the nearest held ground is the prover cluster's geometry engines, and the bridge the synthesis drew to them is priced as shape, not machinery. Questions ran one over the seal (4 against 3): the unread theory would not fold into the ghost question, being a hole of a different shape — not a danger named but a theory named and not yet read — and a skim prices dangers more readily than absences. The genre lesson of the quest now stands complete in its three forms: a silent abstract hides systems (014), an enumerating abstract hides grain (015), and a review's abstract, enumerating a field's joints rather than a result's parts, hides neither — the joints were the count (016). What the skim still cannot price is the weave between the joints, and that is the next refinement the detection list carries: count the debts, not the parts.
# Journal 10 — AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal's Security at the Machine-Code Level

Paper: AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal's Security at the Machine-Code Level
arXiv: Google DeepMind document 36
Absorbed: 2026-11-13

Entry 10 — Paper 017: AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal's Security at the Machine-Code Level (Google DeepMind document 36, 2026-11-10; Swarat Chaudhuri, Andrew Ferraiuolo, Anton Kovsharov, Tej Chajed, Sam Schlesinger, Karn Seth, Jonathan Katz, Charles Sutton, Pushmeet Kohli). Written 2026-11-13, in the same pass as the integration. Will posted it in the MOAT chat in the small hours, with the order to ingest it and to update both the tracker and its phextura shadow — and it is not an arXiv paper at all, but a laboratory's own document, so the blind skim's basis was the paper's own first page: the title and the abstract as they stand on the PDF, sealed by commit before the file was even downloaded whole. Tracker, proof, shadow, score, and this journal from one hand, in that order.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. It arrived by Will's hand, the third such posting in a row, and it landed on the most populated border the tracker owns: on one side cluster Q, the theorem-proving line, whose provers have always worked on models of programs; on the other cluster S, the whole-system studies, which take systems as they stand. This paper's centre of gravity sits between them and in neither — its object is not a theorem and not a survey but an artifact: a deployed binary, proved to behave as its specification says, at the level of the machine code itself. The tracker stood at 205 concepts, fifteen short of the 220 trigger, so an honest-sized delta would carry a restructure pass inside it — the third pass in a row to fall due inside its own paper, if the count ran long.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. The abstract counts in the open, which is the mirror of the failure Paper 014 taught: there, an abstract that declined to count hid its mass; here, the mass is stated in figures — a specification of 677 lines standing for a binary's 5.3 million lines of proof, a forty-fold cut in the lines a proof asks for — and the seal had to price whether stated numbers pre-spend a paper's grain or merely total it. The seal priced them as totals: 11 new concepts (the framework itself; the machine-code level as a chosen ground; the binary implementing the model; the concretization homomorphism; the specification as the tested thing; the amendment protocol; the new protocol named; the ratchet beneath it; proof across rebuilds; judged assumptions; the trusted base); one revision, named in advance, at the translation-verification scroll (1.2.48), which the seal expected this paper to re-read by standing as its second pole; no axis — the slot the paper fills is spanned by the two clusters it stands between, and spanning is not opening; Novelty Index 0.0532; 17 links (11 inside, 6 across); 3 questions; the Oracle to about 80% (70-90), up by the plain band step, on the stated ground that a large novelty read at a low index moves the Oracle by its band and no further, with the confidence split stated — medium on the count, this being the first industrial systems paper the quest has priced, and the genre is not yet calibrated here.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. The numbers were totals, and the count lived in the joints, as it did for the review of Paper 016 — but these joints were not a field's; they were a framework's separations, each one a place where the work refuses to let two things be one. Six separations the seal had folded stood up as concepts of their own: testing a specification (the specification itself fuzzed against the code it describes, before any proof begins); the amendment protocol (the standing path by which the specification may change, with the proofs re-run as the price of change); the gamma bound inside the concretization (the slack the homomorphism is allowed, stated as a number and spent knowingly); the collision clause (refinement claimed only up to collisions, the exception carried in the statement rather than under it); reductions priced as programs (a step of trust charged for the work it does, not merely counted); and the jointness of assumptions (a key used twice judged together, never as two single uses). What the reading added beyond the abstract's frame: the gatekeeper, a role the paper builds into its own process — the one who may hold out the standard a proof must meet, so that the bench cannot teach to the test — and the paper's plainest admission, that the healing of the ratchet after a compromise is not among the properties proved, the keys of the post-quantum protocol being attacker-chosen inside the ratchet's own model. Seven machines were drawn: the session, the ratchet, the amendment, and the gatekeeper's acceptance and the prover team's division of labour, all stated; the command-line run and the security game inferred and marked [synthesis].

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster W founded — the verified artifact, seventeen concepts (1.2.206-1.2.222) — on two foundations (1.3.212-1.3.213: machine code; the compiler) and five defined words in six forms (1.3.214-1.3.218: refinement; homomorphism; prekey, prekeys; vacuity; integrator). One canonical scroll revised, the one the seal named: translation (1.2.48) now carries the rider that it is one of two poles — a compiler proved once for all programs, against an artifact proved once for one binary — and a revised concept is a pole re-read, not a concept replaced. Restructure Pass #11 fell due inside the taking-in, the count reaching 220 at the fifteenth new concept, and moved nothing: the seams between the new cluster and both its neighbours were weighed and held (a prover of models against a prover of artifacts; a study of systems against a proof of one), and the one overlap, at 1.2.48, was refused as a merge candidate on the ground the rider itself states — the paper revises the concept by standing beside it, which is the opposite of absorbing it. Questions Q85-Q88 opened: what keeps a trusted base honest when it outgrows one team's reading; whether the post-quantum healing can be proved at all; whether the collision clause's probability can be fused into the computational accounting; and how much of a binary-level proof is reusable labour. Four questions, one more than the seal priced — the healing question would not fold into any other, being an absence the paper itself flags. The registry stands at 644 entries (True 296, Unknown 348, the False list still empty), drawn to its fixed point, the two drawings byte-identical. The Lean re-proof ran clean on aletheia-core in both the per-paper project and the standing project — currentRegistry_accurate on propext alone in each — over a registry whose count was checked inside the definition at 644 before either build. The shadow took the paper as a native transaction — 33 inserts, 15 replaces, no removes — and came out identical to the direct conversion on all 677 comparable scrolls; the validator stands green (char accounting exact at 64,320 characters, twelve times the pass's 5,360 rewrites; no dangling reference in either layer; the edition checksum verified), and the Novelty Index computed from the transaction log is the canonical Index exactly. The Singular audit closed at residual 0: the field's terms had to be re-cast into the archive's word-forms — possessives of the protocol's nouns given back for plainer shapes, and five of the field's own words (refinement, homomorphism, prekey, vacuity, integrator) carried into the defined-word scrolls, since a word the archive does not hold must be defined in the house or not used in it. The author check, the eighteenth, found no evidence: no signature term anywhere in the full text, the authors' names absent from Will's archive (the one string the search returns is another man's name that merely contains one of theirs), and the work's own ground — a named laboratory, a deployed system, a public record — complete without this house. The pass also kept its own small failures where they belong, in the open: a scroll first read at the wrong coordinate, caught because the dual-maintenance rule sends each pass to the shadow's own table rather than to memory; a file transfer that broke mid-pipe and delivered nothing, caught because the standing registry's hash was checked before its build was trusted; and a heading on the standoff scroll that the registry's classifier reads as its verdict — a wrong heading does not fail loudly, it misclassifies quietly, so the heading is checked as a heading, before the drawing begins.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. On the count, and through the count, on the Index — the quest's largest Index error since Paper 012. Concepts: foretold 11, fell 17. The seal read the abstract's named machinery and folded each mechanism into its nearest neighbour; the reading found the mechanisms were the paper, each one load-bearing and separately named in the work itself. The lesson is the third form of the genre teaching, and it completes a set: a silent abstract hides systems (014); an enumerating abstract hides grain (015); a review's abstract enumerates joints, and the joints were the count (016); an industrial abstract that totals its work in figures hides its separations — the totals are real, and they are sums, and a sum does not show its terms (017). The Index error followed the count exactly: 0.0256 against the sealed 0.0532, the rolling mean at n=10 rising to 0.0166 — the first rise in the mean since the run of exact landings began, and recorded as plainly as they were. Links under-read a fourth paper running (17 foretold, 41 fallen; 36 inside, 5 across): the weave lesson of Paper 016 holds and sharpens — the new concepts cite the held ground they build on at a density no skim has yet priced correctly, and the correction the detection list now carries is mechanical, not a matter of feel: count the debts a founded cluster's concepts owe the scrolls around them, per concept, and price the weave from that. What did not fail: the revision, exact in count and in target, the first revision the quest has named in advance and landed; the axis call, exact — ten of ten, and the first scored call of no-axis, which is the call the streak most needed tested, a run of yeses being evidence of a bias until a no lands among them; and the Oracle, exact in point and range a fifth paper in a row, its rise priced from the band rule rather than from the paper's size, which is why the count's miss did not carry it: the Oracle moves on the Index's band and the shape of the ground, and both of those the seal had right.
# Journal 11 — Isotonic least squares is robust to heteroscedasticity, unimodal least squares is not

Paper: Isotonic least squares is robust to heteroscedasticity, unimodal least squares is not
arXiv: arXiv:2611.17082
Absorbed: 2026-11-13

Entry 11 — Paper 018: Isotonic least squares is robust to heteroscedasticity, unimodal least squares is not (Yannick Baraud, University of Luxembourg; arXiv:2611.17082, math.ST). Written 2026-11-13, in the same pass as the integration. The eighth paper the quest itself picked from a day's listings — and the first picked paper since Paper 013 to land on the tracker's own home ground.

HOW IT WAS CHOSEN. The Friday listings carried 1,264 entries, 665 of them new submissions. The scan worked tracker-proximate first — statistics, probability, optimization, logic, symplectic geometry, dynamics, numerical analysis — and the shortlist held real rivals: a symmetric-programming paper in logic with the reasoning cluster's name on it, a Floer-theory paper over the integers, a paper on interpolation for a modal logic. The pick went to maximal proximity with a crisp stated result: this paper's headline is a claim about an estimator the model already holds a price for — the isotonic least squares estimator of Paper 012, whose sharp rate at the signal stands at 1.2.129 — re-examined under the two hardenings that ground actually meets, noise whose spread changes from point to point and noise too heavy-tailed to square. A paper that re-prices a held object is the quest's best calibration instrument: the skim can be wrong in specific, checkable ways.

WHAT THE SKIM FORESAW. Six new concepts and one revision, Index 0.0277, no axis, Oracle to 90. In substance the skim foresaw the paper's arc whole: the coincidence on the monotone class, the inherited price priced by the average moment rather than the worst point, the unimodal break, and the line case where placement of the noise picks the winner. What it could not see from the abstract was the load-bearing condition — the proof runs through a single assumption about sign steps staying inside the class — and it misread the relationship between the paper's price and the held one.

WHAT THE READING CHANGED. Two things. First, the foretold revision evaporated: the paper's bound prices the l1-risk of the estimator by degree and average moment, where the held scroll prices its l2-risk by the signal's local variation — a second price standing beside the first, not over it. Nothing in 1.2.129 is touched, because its driver is untouched. Second, the condition split out: the sign-step assumption is the whole engine of the coincidence, stated for every class that meets it, and the tracker gives such instruments their own scrolls — so the count ran to seven, not six.

WHAT THE INTEGRATION DID TO THE MODEL. Cluster O, founded by Paper 010's statistics and deepened by Papers 012 and 013, now holds a line it did not hold before: for least squares, robustness is not a property of the estimator but of the pair (estimator, class) — the monotone cone's shape gives a loud reading nowhere to stand, the one-peak class's free peak gives it exactly the room it needs, and on a line the deciding variable is alignment, where the noise stands against where the shape stands large. Three questions opened on that line (Q94-Q96), Q56's noise side was answered in passing, and the pass also repaired two upkeep debts the tracker had been carrying quietly: the Research Oracle detection list had no Sign Two paragraphs for Papers 016-018 and no Sign Three entry for Paper 017, and the registry framing scroll had no drawing paragraph for Study S010. All stand written now. One defect of this pass's own making deserves its line: a branch-order slip in the registry extractor briefly mis-sourced Study S010's seven concepts to this paper; the canonical checks did not catch it, and the phextura shadow's warrant diff did — seven concept replaces that should not exist, each pointing at the wrong bibliography entry. The dual maintenance earned its keep as an error-detector, not only as a copy.

WHERE THE PREDICTION FAILED, AND WHY. The revision, and it failed in a new shape — the fifth phantom. The skim's rule of thumb, 'same estimator, new noise, therefore the held price is re-measured', treats the priced thing as the estimator alone. The priced thing is a triple: estimator, risk, driver. Two of the three changed, so nothing was re-measured; a parallel price was built. The count error ran the other way and is the older lesson recurring: the instrument is a concept, not a detail of the theorem it serves. Against those, the structural calls all landed — axis eleven for eleven, Oracle point and range exact for the sixth weighing in a row, questions and cross-cluster links to the count — and the Index error, 0.0020, is the smallest since Paper 016's exact landing. The rolling mean over the last ten scored papers stands at 0.0159.
